## The idea

On a trek, water from streams and lakes is often boiled before drinking. Up high, water boils at a lower temperature, so cooking takes longer and germs need more time to be killed. This feature gives two simple answers for any altitude.

## How it works

- **Boiling point:** it falls by about 1 °C for every 300 m of altitude. At 0 m it is 100 °C, at 3,000 m about 90 °C and at 4,500 m about 85 °C. This is a good guide up to about 5,000 m.
- **Boiling time for safe water:** following the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), keep water at a rolling boil for 1 minute, or 3 minutes above about 2,000 m (6,500 ft).

## Where the code lives

- `packages/core/src/boilingPoint.ts`: the two functions (pure maths, works offline).
- `packages/core/test/boilingPoint.test.ts`: automated tests that check the examples above.

## Limits

This is a tested building block. It does not appear on a screen yet; the team can connect it later, for example in the safety card. Boiling only deals with germs. It does not remove chemicals. The boiling point is an estimate, not a measurement.

